      "writtenOn": "2023-08-29T19:28:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Shouldn\u0027t we free the node we tried to insert in case of any error? Not just if it already exists?",
      "revId": "cb93b7f618c844e066415c8b99f37c4ae106744e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "22b87f01_e23e85bf",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kernel/module-plts.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 91,
      "author": {
        "id": 1988540
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-09-07T01:53:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good catch! For other errors, we definitely need to free the node. But do we still return true? This is yet another place where I am not entirely convinced of considering as a duplicate. I am not sure if its norm in the kernel to ignore errors which are highly unlikely.\n\nIn this case, on inspecting the rhashtable_lookup_insert_fast function, it seems to have quite a lot of different possible return values. E2BIG, EEXIST, EAGAIN, ENOMEM to name some. So before I change this, I need some guidance on \n1. Would returning true be alright in case of an error?\n2. If a comment is needed here, and if yes, what explanation would be needed.",
      "parentUuid": "0a4322f5_50756ada",
      "revId": "cb93b7f618c844e066415c8b99f37c4ae106744e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "890f3904_2cb68cdb",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kernel/module-plts.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 91,
      "author": {
        "id": 1934715
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-09-21T20:28:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Following the idea of letting the error handling kick in later, counting any error as a duplicate would make sense. I do think a comment is needed here, but you\u0027re already going to have a comment explaining why we treat a memory allocation failure as a duplicate, so perhaps it makes more sense to add a comment to the function itself (i.e. add a comment above the function signature) explaining why it handles failures the way it does.",
      "parentUuid": "22b87f01_e23e85bf",
      "revId": "cb93b7f618c844e066415c8b99f37c4ae106744e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7b00f4bf_39770be9",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kernel/module-plts.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 106,
      "author": {
        "id": 1934715
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-29T19:28:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This could potentially be called twice in the following sequence:\n\n1. Load module (results in one call to `module_arch_freeing_init()` from `do_init_module()`)\n2. Unload module (results in one call to `module_arch_freeing_init()` from `free_module()`)\n\nSince `mod-\u003earch.[core|init].elf64_rela_rht` isn\u0027t cleared in step #1, they are left as dangling pointers. This means during step 2, you will do a double free.\n\nYou can avoid this by NULL\u0027ing out the `elf64_rela_rht` pointers after freeing them the first time.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 106,
        "startChar": 23,
        "endLine": 106,
        "endChar": 37
      },
      "revId": "cb93b7f618c844e066415c8b99f37c4ae106744e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
